---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import BrowseSidebar from "../../components/BrowseSidebar.astro";
import { GITHUB_GAME_DATA } from "../../data/github-game-data.js";
const source = `https://github.com/openfrontio/OpenFrontIO/blob/${GITHUB_GAME_DATA.revision}/src/core/configuration/Config.ts`;
---
<Layout title="Nuclear Defense Guide — OpenFront Wiki" description="A practical OpenFront guide to SAM coverage, missile silos, MIRVs, and surviving nuclear escalation."><SiteHeader /><div class="mx-auto grid max-w-7xl gap-8 px-4 py-8 sm:px-6 lg:grid-cols-[230px_minmax(0,1fr)]"><aside class="lg:sticky lg:top-20 lg:h-[calc(100vh-6rem)] lg:overflow-y-auto" data-pagefind-ignore><details open><summary class="mb-3 flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-cyan-glow lg:cursor-default">Browse Pages <span class="lg:hidden">▾</span></summary><BrowseSidebar /></details></aside><main class="min-w-0"><nav class="mb-6 flex items-center gap-2 text-xs text-dawn/50" data-pagefind-ignore><a href="/" class="hover:text-aquarius">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/category/advanced-mechanics" class="hover:text-aquarius">Advanced Mechanics</a><span>/</span><span class="text-dawn">Nuclear defense</span></nav><header class="border-b border-white/10 pb-8"><p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-cyan-glow">Player guide</p><h1 class="mt-2 font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-wide text-white sm:text-4xl">Nuclear defense</h1><p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-dawn/80">Treat nuclear defense as coverage, warning time, and economic triage. A SAM is useful only if it covers the threat and remains available when the payload arrives.</p></header><article class="wiki-content mt-8" data-pagefind-body><div class="guide-callout"><strong>Do not defend everything equally</strong><p>Protect the structures and approaches that keep your economy and army alive. A small, overlapping defensive network around the decisive area is usually more useful than scattered launchers with no reserve.</p></div><h2>Know the current SAM envelope</h2><p>The current source lists a 90-tick SAM cooldown, 300-tick construction time, default range 70, and upgrade ceiling 150. The ranges are source values; the actual interception situation also depends on trajectory and the incoming projectile.</p><h2>Respect the MIRV economy</h2><p>A MIRV starts at 25,000,000 gold and adds 15,000,000 for each global launch. That scaling makes a first launch strategically important and makes repeated launches increasingly expensive. Keep enough economy to rebuild coverage rather than spending every reserve on a speculative response.</p><h2>Practical response</h2><ol><li>Identify the likely launch direction and move critical assets inside overlapping coverage.</li><li>Keep SAMs upgraded where the extra range protects a real approach.</li><li>Do not assume a carrier and its deployed warheads behave identically; read the current MIRV article and source rules.</li><li>After an interception or launch, account for cooldown before claiming an area is safe.</li><li>Use the troop recovery band and economy guide together: nuclear defense that empties your army can still lose the match.</li></ol><h2>Related reading</h2><p><a href="/SAM_Launcher">SAM Launcher</a>, <a href="/MIRV">MIRV</a>, <a href="/Missile_Silo">Missile Silo</a>, <a href="/Atom_Bomb">Atom Bomb</a>, and <a href="/reference/nuclear-systems">Nuclear Systems</a>.</p><p class="source-note">Current costs, cooldowns, and ranges checked against <a href={source} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenFrontIO Config.ts ↗</a>.</p></article></main></div><SiteFooter /></Layout>